-- title:
--   Lifting separable residue points on smooth schemes over henselian DVRs
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a henselian local domain which is a discrete valuation ring, with residue field $\kappa =$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField R`, let $X$ be a scheme and let $f \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ be a smooth morphism. Let $k'$ be a field which is a finite-dimensional separable $\kappa$-algebra, and let $x_0 \colon \operatorname{Spec} k' \to X$ be a point whose composite with $f$ is the morphism induced by the ring map $R \to \kappa \to k'$ (the residue map followed by the structure map of $k'$). The conclusion asserts the existence of a type $R'$ carrying a commutative ring structure making it a domain and a discrete valuation ring, together with an $R$-algebra structure for which $R'$ is a finite $R$-module, étale over $R$, faithfully flat as an $R$-module, with $R \to R'$ a local homomorphism, and $R'$ henselian local; furthermore a $\kappa$-algebra isomorphism $\varphi \colon k' \simeq$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField R'` and a morphism $s \colon \operatorname{Spec} R' \to X$, such that the ideal generated by the image of the maximal ideal of $R$ is the maximal ideal of $R'$, $s$ followed by $f$ equals the morphism induced by $R \to R'$, and the closed immersion $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,R') \to \operatorname{Spec} R'$ followed by $s$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\varphi$ followed by $x_0$.
--
--   This is the finite-level form, over a henselian (not necessarily strictly henselian) discrete valuation ring, of the statement that points of a smooth scheme in a finite separable extension of the residue field lift to sections after a finite étale local base change, as in EGA IV 17.16.3. It is used in the variant [`AlgebraicGeometry.Smooth.exists_finite_etale_dvr_specMap_comp_eq_apply_closedPoint_mem_of_henselianLocalRing`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Smooth.exists_finite_etale_dvr_specMap_comp_eq_apply_closedPoint_mem_of_henselianLocalRing), where the lifted section is additionally controlled at the closed point.
